WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.102
The main lesson is simple: TypeScript helps structure code, but runtime validation is still required for external input.

2
00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:15.732
Types improve the developer experience and catch many mistakes before deployment, yet they disappear at runtime.

3
00:00:15.732 --> 00:00:25.172
Any value that crosses a trust boundary should be treated as unknown until the application has checked its shape, format, limits, and context.

4
00:00:25.172 --> 00:00:30.678
That mindset prevents static types from being confused with security validation.

5
00:00:30.678 --> 00:00:34.296
Strong validation starts by identifying boundaries.

6
00:00:34.296 --> 00:00:48.928
Request bodies, parameters, headers, cookies, browser storage, WebSocket messages, postMessage events, environment variables, configuration files, queues, files, and SDK responses can all carry surprising data.

7
00:00:48.928 --> 00:00:55.614
At those boundaries, schemas and guards should parse unknown values into trusted internal objects.

8
00:00:55.614 --> 00:01:04.660
Libraries such as Zod, Joi, Yup, and Ajv can help, especially when teams keep schemas, inferred types, tests, and API contracts aligned.

9
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:09.773
Good validation also distinguishes transport shape from business meaning.

10
00:01:09.773 --> 00:01:17.482
A payload may be structurally valid while still violating ownership, workflow, date, money, or cross-field rules.

11
00:01:17.482 --> 00:01:22.123
Transport validators should reject malformed and unexpected input.

12
00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:28.258
Domain validation should protect invariants that must remain true inside the application.

13
00:01:28.258 --> 00:01:37.226
That separation makes the code easier to reason about and reduces the chance that an API convenience becomes a business logic weakness.

14
00:01:37.226 --> 00:01:39.743
Finally, validation needs evidence.

15
00:01:39.743 --> 00:01:50.362
Negative tests, boundary tests, malformed input tests, contract tests, and focused code review questions all help confirm that the rules work under pressure.

16
00:01:50.362 --> 00:01:57.127
Safe error responses and careful logging make failures useful without exposing unnecessary detail.

17
00:01:57.127 --> 00:02:03.106
In practical TypeScript engineering, the goal is not to validate everything everywhere.

18
00:02:03.106 --> 00:02:10.342
The goal is to make runtime data trustworthy before it reaches the typed application logic that matters most.
